  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Simplified family MUAC integrated nutrition program in Taiz</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mercy Corps proposes adoption/ further testing of the uniMUAC bracelet for use by caregivers. Early laboratory tests show it improves on options such as the UNICEF bracelet and clickMUAC, with higher specificity, features to aid use by mothers, and additional measurement for pregnant and lactating women (PLWs) who remain underserved by existing options. This intervention will target nutrition outcomes, but will also impact health, food security, and social cohesion. MUAC is considered a strong indicator for nutrition-related mortality in children.  It is easily used by caregivers with lower literacy and is low-cost. Districts in Taiz carry a heavy burden of malnutrition. This innovation will target communities further from health facilities, allowing them to monitor nutrition status at home. Distribution will layer with existing programming in the same district. Selected Community Health Volunteers (CHVs) from Governorate Health Office (GHO) lists will deliver and educate on the bracelets and provide follow up visits. In order to add to the evidence base, it will be implemented using geographic clusters to compare the uniMUAC and UNICEF bracelets as a first in a field setting.</narrative>
  </description>
